---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { kbdTree } from "../../../demos/kbd";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";

/* The skin is already global (Base.astro imports it for the ⌘K badge), so this page needs no import. */

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;

---

<Base title="Kbd" description="Kbd: a rendered key using the native <kbd>, styling hooks, and a React wrapper.">
  <h1>Kbd</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Kbd is a <strong>drawn key</strong>: the native <code>&lt;kbd&gt;</code> with the appearance of a
    physical key. You use it to show a shortcut, the ⌘K in the search engine above, the Esc at the bottom of a
    <a href="/en/components/command-palette">CommandPalette</a>. It is static, like Badge: stateless, without machine, without vanilla enhancer. The semantics of
    <code>&lt;kbd&gt;</code> is from the platform; The component only provides the look.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview tree={kbdTree} label="Kbd" />

  <h2>A glyph reads square</h2>
  <p>
    A single key (<kbd class="sk-kbd">K</kbd>, <kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘</kbd>, <kbd class="sk-kbd">↑</kbd>) takes a least square instead of being a sliver; a longer tag (<kbd class="sk-kbd">Esc</kbd>, <kbd class="sk-kbd">Enter</kbd>) grows with its text. The minimum is <code>--sk-kbd-min-size</code>, relative to the key's own font, so it remains square at any size.
  </p>

  <h2>The pressed state</h2>
  <p>
    Kbd is not a control, it is not clickable, so its only state <strong>reflects</strong> an external event: <code>data-pressed</code>, which turns it on while its physical key is pressed, just as a component reflects the <code>data-state</code> of a machine. It is written by someone looking at the keyboard, not the kbd. When turned on, the key becomes accented, sinks a pixel and loses its shadow, like a real key that goes down. The transition uses the <code>feedback</code> intent ( <a href="/en/motion">motion</a> ).
  </p>
  <p>
    Try it: press any of these and it turns on by itself; hold <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="meta"
 >⌘</kbd> and add another to see the combination.
  </p>
  <div class="key-echo" role="group" aria-label="Keys that react to the keyboard">
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="meta">⌘</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="control">Ctrl</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="shift">⇧</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="k">K</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="escape">Esc</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="enter">↵</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="arrowup">↑</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="arrowdown">↓</kbd>
    <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-key="/in">/</kbd>
  </div>
  <p>
    An entire chord also, as a unit, presses <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-hotkey="mod+enter"
 >⌘ ↵</kbd> and is turned on when the entire combination is down:
  </p>
  <p>
    It is set by a small script from the doc while the physical key is pressed; for the chord it uses the <code>matchesHotkey</code> of the <a href="/en/hotkey">hotkey primitive</a>, the same matcher as the shortcut. The ⌘K badge of the search engine above comes from that pair: <code>formatHotkey</code> gives the text by platform, Kbd gives it the box.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>
  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "components/kbd.css", probe: "sk-kbd" }]} />
</Base>

<style>
  .key-echo {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-inline-sm);
    align-items: center;
    margin-block: var(--space-stack-md);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initKeyEcho } from "../../../scripts/key-echo";
  initKeyEcho();
</script>
